To cancel a fetch request, you need to create an `AbortController`, assign its `AbortSignal` to the request's `signal` property, and then call the controller's `abort()` method when you want to cancel the request.

Here's an example of how to do it, as shown on the page [5]:
```javascript
const controller = new AbortController();

const fetchButton = document.querySelector("#fetch");
fetchButton.addEventListener("click", async () => {
  try {
    console.log("Starting fetch");
    const response = await fetch("https://example.org/get", {
      signal: controller.signal,
    });
    console.log(`Response: ${response.status}`);
  } catch (e) {
    console.error(`Error: ${e}`);
  }
});

const cancelButton = document.querySelector("#cancel");
cancelButton.addEventListener("click", () => {
  controller.abort();
  console.log("Canceled fetch");
});
```
Alternatively, you can create a request from another request, while changing some of its properties using the second argument, as shown on the page [4]:
```javascript
async function post(request) {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(request);
    const result = await response.json();
    console.log("Success:", result);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("Error:", error);
  }
}

const request1 = new Request("https://example.org/post", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: {
    "Content-Type": "application/json",
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({ username: "example1" }),
});

const request2 = new Request(request1, {
  body: JSON.stringify({ username: "example2" }),
});

post(request1);
post(request2);
```
Note that if the request is aborted after the `fetch()` call has been fulfilled but before the response body has been read, then attempting to read the response body will reject with an `AbortError` exception, as shown on the page [6].

To check whether the response was successful before reading it, you can check the `ok` property of the `Response` object, which returns `true` if the status is in the 200 range, as stated in [3] [4]. 

Here's how you can do it:

```javascript
async function getData() {
  const url = "https://example.org/products.json";
  try {
    const response = await fetch(url);
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`Response status: ${response.status}`);
    }
    // Read the response body as JSON
    const result = await response.json();
    console.log(result);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error.message);
  }
}
```

Alternatively, you can check the `ok` property before reading the response body, as shown in [3] [4]. 

```javascript
async function getData() {
  const url = "https://example.org/products.json";
  try {
    const response = await fetch(url);
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`Response status: ${response.status}`);
    }
    // Check the response type
    const type = response.type;
    // Check the headers
    const contentType = response.headers.get("content-type");
    // Read the response body as JSON
    const result = await response.json();
    console.log(result);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error.message);
  }
}
```

Note that the page also mentions that if the server responds with an error like 404, `fetch()` will fulfill with a `Response`, so you should also check the status before reading the response body, as shown in [1].

To encode form data as the request body, you can use a `URLSearchParams` object to encode the data, and then pass it as the `body` option in the `fetch()` function.

Here's an example:
```javascript
const params = new URLSearchParams();
params.append("username", "example");

const response = await fetch(`https://example.org/login?${params}`);
```
This will send a GET request with the encoded form data as a query string.

Alternatively, you can create a `FormData` object and pass it as the `body` option:
```javascript
const formData = new FormData();
formData.append("username", "example");

const response = await fetch("https://example.org/login", {
  method: "POST",
  body: formData,
});
```
Note that in this case, we're using a POST request instead of a GET request, since the `FormData` object is being used as the request body.

The page also mentions that you can use `JSON.stringify()` to convert an object to a string, and then pass it as the `body` option:
```javascript
const response = await fetch("https://example.org/post", {
  method: "POST",
  body: JSON.stringify({ username: "example" }),
});
```
However, this will only work if the server expects a JSON payload, and the `Content-Type` header is set to `application/json`.

All of these examples are mentioned in the page excerpts above, specifically in sections [1], [2], [4], and [5].
